      "locked": false,
      "text": "                   \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                          Unit of CSS\nIt is used to define the size of an element and help us to control the\nlayout and finishing out layout.\n\nThere are three types of units :\n\n1. Absolute Unit : An absolute unit in CSS is a fixed measured unit that\ndoes not change based on the screen size, parent element, or user brower\npreferences.\nex : px, mm, cm, etc...\n\n2. Relative Unit : Relative units in CSS calculate dimensions based on another\nexternal value, such as a parent element's size, the root element's font size,\nor the viemport's dimensions.\nex : %, em (element), rem(root element), vh, vw, etc...\n\n3. Viewport Unit : CSS viewport units are relative length measurements based entirely\non the dimensions of the brower's visible window area (the viewport).\nex : vw (viewport width), vh(view heigth), vmin, vmax, etc... ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                   \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                          Unit of CSS\nIt is used to define the size of an element and help us to control the\nlayout and finishing out layout.\n\nThere are three types of units :\n\n1. Absolute Unit : An absolute unit in CSS is a fixed measured unit that\ndoes not change based on the screen size, parent element, or user brower\npreferences.\nex : px, mm, cm, etc...\n\n2. Relative Unit : Relative units in CSS calculate dimensions based on another\nexternal value, such as a parent element's size, the root element's font size,\nor the viemport's dimensions.\nex : %, em (element), rem(root element), vh, vw, etc...\n\n3. Viewport Unit : CSS viewport units are relative length measurements based entirely\non the dimensions of the brower's visible window area (the viewport).\nex : vw (viewport width), vh(view heigth), vmin, vmax, etc... ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
